inline.s:
Seven marshes command Jing and Xiang;
one long water joins with the sky.
The river’s flow turns back at Red Cliff;
the hills’ hues embrace Huanggang.
For rhapsodies, the Fisherman is praised;
in walking songs, Chu’s madman is recalled.
I have always longed to wash my feet —
and now I am already in the Canglang.

This mountain was my dwelling in a former life;
this forest opens from today.
Erring, I was snared by dust and toil and went away;
rejoicing, I come back by grace of an old bond.
Bones stand stark as these lean ridged peaks;
mind at leisure, a hundred thoughts turned ash.
Mist and cloud are now enough for me —
why should I ask after Mount Tiantai?

Fleeing the world brings no gloom at heart;
dwelling in mountains, I never tire of depth.
Thick clouds bring rain though the sky is clear;
in hidden ravines the day keeps its shade.
Fawns sleep in the thick grass;
crows gather home to the evening wood.
The bright moon falls upon the peak
and shatters a lifetime’s heart.

The mist and cloud were my old sworn lord;
coming back, I am straightway home.
Clouds rise like bolts of white silk;
hills gather round like a lotus.
Deep sleep forgets both dusk and dawn;
dull-witted Chan watches the years pass.
Here is ground where I may set my feet —
no cassock is needed for it.

White hair reflects a withered face;
I hide my form, and for that I have the hills.
The rest of life waits only to dissolve;
one breath, and all returns to ease.
My Chan gone stale, I find it hard to speak;
clouds are deep, and the gate is easily shut.
Perfect penetration enters the flowing water,
murmuring day and night.

Age and idleness suit each other well;
the body wastes, the breath no longer holds.
Seeing and hearing seem all a dream;
rising or sitting, I am suddenly a fool.
Sun and moon go on from dusk to dawn;
bloom and wither, let the seasons have their way.
All that remains is a single thought —
silently entering no-thought.

The night sky clears, and I am glad;
autumn comes, fresh coolness rises.
After rain the forest leaves hang heavy;
wind passes, the ridge clouds lighten.
In still contemplation I see no-self;
hidden in practice, I tire of fame.
Sitting, I watch the moon in the empty realm,
distinct, facing my solitary brightness.

Ten thousand sounds are hushed;
the mind’s source is clear as water.
Censer smoke is fine through the night;
mountain moonlight brightens the window.
Insects rest easy in the grass;
the cloud-sleeping crane is unstartled.
Sitting deep, all thoughts die out —
suddenly I hear the dawn bell.

The heat need not be refused;
coolness surely has its hour.
Clouds fly, the mountain hue falls;
thunder moves, and rain follows.
Grudge not your thin hemp robe its weight;
blame not the autumn wind for being late.
Just sit beneath the pine
and wait for a good wind to blow.

Sitting bolt upright, I closely watch the mind;
its source is not easy to find.
When it stirs, the first omens appear;
where it rises, all is profoundly hidden.
In the still silence, a sound struck in emptiness;
endlessly, the chirp of a fledgling comes forth.
Know that the mark beyond thought
belongs neither to floating nor to sinking.

The gathering of suffering is the cause of birth;
this body of great affliction is hard to dissolve.
What holds it up is only bone;
its movements are not mine to command.
One breath is thin as a thread;
the remnant frame is tiny as dust.
All along it has been but a loan —
in the end, to whom is it dear?

In deep autumn the cold grows heavy;
wrapping the robe close suits me well.
A man grown old, his bones are only stronger;
a pine gone withered, its branches more strange.
The yellow chrysanthemum’s will to live is faint;
white hair is far from worldly feeling.
Sitting alone, all conditions forgotten —
in the vast silence, only I know.

Having crossed the world’s end, still I journey far;
through a hundred Yue mists I count no miles.
Only in peril do I learn how wide the dust-sea is;
at the road’s end I see birth and death as light.
For a while I rest in the light of water and moon;
by chance I walk on ground of lapis lazuli.
The boat that reached the shore is now cast off —
for whom do the upper hall’s bells and drums sound?

In the world the gourd is useless and most things go wrong;
so by the river’s heart I mean to still the workings of my mind.
There is a temple, yet it will not let a monk stay even a while;
having no home, I should race the crane to return.
The cloud of compassion secretly covers the room of the empty-born;
fragrant mist idly soaks a passing traveler’s robe.
A precious raft has hung for a thousand ages over the ford of delusion —
if the swift current would but stop, one would take refuge at once.

From afar I ask Caoxi about the mirror stand;
entering the gate, at one glance our faces break into smiles.
Your body arrives with idle clouds from Mount Lu;
your heart brings the white snow of Mount Yan.
You have journeyed to the end of birth and death, a road beyond the sky;
your greetings spell out all the plum blossoms on the ridge.
If an old friend’s thought could be like yours,
this mind of mine would long ago have turned to ash.

Mist and cloud were always far from the dust of the world;
that I longed for woods and springs, dare I take as true?
Grown old, my mind is like a tree without fire;
what life remains is like a second spring.
Heaven and earth will not permit those who flee to Chan;
the rites and law are not forgotten by one who has left the world.
Only the apes and cranes, companions of the empty hills,
come often in clear dreams to keep this idle body company.

Court and rivers-and-lakes face different skies;
that we meet at all cannot be a bond of this one life.
In office, you wield compassion well;
meeting the world, you set the mind at ease in free and easy Chan.
Your salary alone is fit to pave the ground;
no trace of dust is left to follow you into sleep.
The udumbara bloomed once in three thousand years —
I rejoice it opens again by the Northern Dipper.

Days of rain and cloud, the day will not clear;
at the thatched window I am glad an old friend comes.
Pine blossoms are set aside to brew, brows knit the while;
in the Lotus Society, who would decline a rhapsodist’s gift?
The world’s affairs I only see as finger and horse;
this mind, I will not liken to cold ashes.
Sitting, I hear the flute on the city wall at dusk;
gust after gust of light wind carries the falling plum.

Wind and rain sigh over the boat on the river;
drifting, I have just learned to roam in emptiness.
Waking from a dream, cloud-cranes rest on the pine tops;
at leisure I watch gulls play on the sand.
No need to trouble the north-flying geese with letters;
worldly feeling has long been given to the eastward flow.
Two-thirds of a hundred years are gone;
whatever life remains is only drifting.

The north wind drives the rain and darkens the mountain town;
at the year’s end, at the world’s edge, I am still on a far campaign.
To flee the world I would follow the herd of deer;
on a fearful road the partridge’s cry breaks the heart.
Ten years in the miasmic seas, a lone tumbleweed turning;
one night, frost flowers have grown at both temples.
I whip my horse through mud to a lodging in the wild —
I cannot bear to look back at the evening mist.

Years ago, with a single bowl, we kept long company;
through long nights, robed against the cold, we listened for the dawn bell.
At every meal I think of the realm of Fragrant Accumulation;
walking in meditation, I remember always the Wondrous Peak.
Quietly the fever of miasma melts — the heart holds snow;
silently I count the years by pines my own hands planted.
Sweep the lion seat of the lotus for me,
and wait till I once more raise the teaching beyond words.

How like the Jetavana is this sitting in the banana grove;
I borrow the clear wind to loosen my cares awhile.
Green leaves have often served Huaisu’s brush;
deep shade still covers the sutra-translating porch. (There is the Hall of Sutra Translation and Brush Transmission of Master Fang.)
Out of reverence for life, no morning bowl-round;
to practice samādhi, one should shut the door by day.
I have heard that originally there is not a single thing —
and so all day I sit facing wordlessness.

To inquire into the upward matter, I visited Caoxi;
the matter is clear — from the first, one is not deluded.
In the dawn courtyard the wind rises and blows the green bamboo;
after rain on the spring hills, the staff grows green again.
Only at leisure do I feel all conditions grow still;
only after awakening do I know the ten thousand things are one.
Most intimate is the thing that calls a man awake:
at the fifth watch, a dream broken by a single crowing cock.

That single blow of old divided sage from commoner;
the true, sure pivot dissolves both sight and hearing.
For a thousand autumns incense holds the royal aura;
through all ages the rivers and hills are covered by the cloud of compassion.
In the empty wood the deer still follow the whisk;
the Pure Land’s lotus is already yours.
Leaning on my staff in the spring wind, I stand and linger;
the setting sun’s purple and emerald hang thick with mist.

A whole year of kindred spirits — many a thought it cost;
we meet with joy as the year runs out.
A little boat through a snowy night, a thousand li;
a single branch of Pure Land lotus planted.
The body grown old is only so much lumber;
the days and months ahead are new acquaintance.
Say not that Mount Kuang’s old society is no more —
that it will open again in this place may surely be hoped.

West of Shravasti lies the ancient ground of the Way;
by chance I pass and walk three times around, bowing to the King of Emptiness.
Contemplating mind, I have already entered the mind-only land;
to expound the Dharma I ascend the Hall of Good Dharma.
Fragrant rice can fill the many with contentment;
the ghee is tasted only by the keenest faculties.
The host does not lightly hold it out at once —
if I might come again, I would raise it once more.

The Jetavana opens west of the great river;
a lotus wells from the earth, the best place to rest.
The Buddha-land stands far beyond all marks;
the Dharma-body rises as high as the four heavens.
If I come but briefly, you bid me mount the flower seat;
if I stay long, I should often lean on this staff.
A pity that, visiting, I must say farewell —
I cannot bear to look back, so bleak the heart.

The Plum-Snow Hall opens, and the bones grow purer still;
after the meal I stroll and walk in meditation once.
Fragrance floats in the stone chamber where the flowers first open;
shadows enter the ice-jar, and the moon is doubly bright.
Before the rock of the severed arm an old trace remains;
in the flute that mourns a friend I recall a new sound.
If you would ask today the meaning of the West’s coming —
who can tell the journey of that single shoe?

The Brahma-king’s palace hides in mist and cloud;
outside the gate, red dust and the world’s road stretch far.
The mountain opens a Pure Land from its nine peaks;
monks sit on lotuses of a thousand petals.
Light floats in the stone chamber, where imperial writing remains;
shadows fall on all the heavens, guarding the crimson gauze.
If you would ask the true meaning of the West’s coming —
it is plainly all entrusted to a single cassock.

From afar I bow at the Dharma altar of the famous mountain;
this mind must beg the Patriarch for its peace.
The night moon over the nine peaks whitens a man through;
pine wind from ten thousand gorges chills to the bone.
The lamp of wisdom, nearly out, flares up again;
the robe and bowl are left — to whom shall they be passed?
You should know the day when the heavenly lord took refuge:
the lion’s voice has not yet ceased to speak.

I visit the garden in the suburbs by the Han River’s bank;
it is just like the gathering at Vaiśālī.
We eat our fill of fragrant rice, an offering from heaven;
I delight in subtle words, the songs of Ying.
The level fields were once the model for a monk’s robe;
tall willows newly droop their dew-sprinkled branches.
Wind and rain in the deep night, the mind’s realm still —
cool as though sitting in a pool of lotus flowers.

In a thicket of thorns stands an ancient ground of the Way;
twenty years of toil — for whom was all the busyness?
The hall opens, and dragons and elephants come from the four seas;
three times a day, chanting, they venerate the Dharma King.
Within its bounds the Avataṃsaka realm is perfected;
between the brows the white-hair light shines always.
Looking up in reliance, one has entered the mind-only land —
what need to raise the upward matter again?

A blue lotus rising from water — a heart that dwells in the world;
your carriage and cap are yet as though among woods and hills.
Empty flowers and mirror images — how still the dust;
a lone moon on the cold river, the meaning deeper still.
I think you received your prophecy at Vulture Peak;
in Vaiśālī you must be an old kindred spirit.
We meet, and speak a single word of the un-born;
face to face it is clear — no need to seek.

Alone I climb the high peak, leaning on my staff;
the encroaching empty-green grows bleak and bewildering.
The two eyes of the West’s coming are like a peng’s wings;
eastward, a thousand hills are like horse hooves.
The sheer gorge has long been called a lion’s den;
the empty wood may yet let the elephant king rest.
And now, if I would speak the Dharma of the un-born —
the whisk is barely raised, and the ten thousand forms bow low.

Long ago at Caoxi I formed a noble bond;
since we parted, the Patriarch’s Chan has grown cold and quiet.
When we speak of non-duality, I think of Vimalakīrti;
whenever we talk of the un-born, I remember Danian.
You trust that a magistrate’s office is a manifestation;
who would know that official papers are the true exposition?
The moon before the withered-wood hall on Snow Peak —
tonight, because of you, it wanes and waxes full again.

I have traveled all the rivers and lakes and long grown weary of roaming;
to the green hills I come at last, only when old.
The body has already fallen away, a shadow of empty flowers;
the world’s affairs all follow the flowing water.
In the still silence this idle body takes the clouds for company;
with a sigh, white hair is covered over with snow.
No need to reckon how many years remain:
one thought of the un-born, and vast kalpas rest.

I remember the White Society of Mount Kuang in those days;
this day’s lofty carriage is most worth recalling.
Where one enters, one can at once forget the world’s cares;
arriving here, there is no need at all to knit the brow.
The body is wrapped in cloud-damp from ten thousand gorges;
I sit waiting while the moonlight lags on a thousand peaks.
One night of clear talk becomes a noble trace;
heaven and earth have always prized the heart’s understanding.

I left home as a boy and do not remember the house;
looking back on things past, all is cause for sighing.
Old friends still picture the face of a child;
a withered tree can hardly open its former flowers.
By the river the willow branches droop with morning dew;
before the gate the mountain hues carry the dawn glow.
Only this scene remains, year after year —
no need to ask the past about the passing years.

Twenty years of diligent company in the miasmic land;
again with bowl and staff I roam the many quarters.
To inquire into the mystery I climbed straight to the Golden Wheel’s summit;
entering the room, I still rely on the great Dharma hall.
To go home to the mountain is indeed my wish;
but springs and stones in old age — how could I forget them?
If the years left me are not yet cast into the ditches,
I wait for you to dwell together in the field of stillness.

Your coming suddenly recalls an old friend’s love;
in the end I cannot forget the bond of leaving the world.
At first sight of his bequeathed words, it is as though we faced each other;
thinking back on his talk and laughter, it seems many lives ago.
I know he dwells on a high seat in Tuṣita;
entering straight into bodhi, how should he reckon the journey?
If we meet again as in former days,
would I let a fellow traveler walk a separate road?

From afar you ask after the deer and boars of the empty hills;
your covered cart enters the valley at slanting dusk.
Robes open, we speak softly of what lies in the heart;
brewing tea and burning incense, we sit past midnight.
Glad in the bright moon, the mind is like a mirror;
deeply observing the world’s affairs, they are suddenly as clouds.
If, at the moment, you ask the meaning of the West’s coming —
there is not a single thing I can hand to you.

All my life my tracks have followed former karma;
I am ashamed this bodily frame is not yet free.
One piece of idle mind shows itself wherever I am;
for no reason white hair shifts in the dark.
I know the days to come are all days of subtraction;
who believes that adding years is losing them?
When I look back and go home to the mountain,
I shall sleep on a stone, high-pillowed on ten thousand peaks.

The layman has always manifested a responsive body;
his vajra mind-ground is pure, without a speck of dust.
Long has he awakened to the patient acceptance of the un-born;
dwelling in the world, he is yet as one who has left it.
Vimalakīrti’s Dharma gate is not silence alone;
Layman Pang’s wife and children were not his kin.
His spirit has already entered the lotus treasury —
why ask the great chun tree about the count of kalpas?

Looking back on the five turbidities, the air hangs thick;
the noisy crowd buzzes like mosquitoes in a bowl.
Before a thought flashes, delusion and awakening are cut off;
the moment a mote stands, sage and commoner divide.
The green hills may allow me to hide my clumsiness;
but who can save the burning house from its fire?
I lift my head to the long sky with its pair of blue eyes —
I cannot bear that the whole earth is only floating cloud.

How sad — past things were wanderings in a dream;
flowers in a clouded eye cannot be sought.
The mind’s road is truly like the moon when clouds disperse;
the body, let it be as froth floating on water.
While life holds one breath, three inches still remain;
to enter a thousand peaks in old age is worth ten gains.
From now on my traces in the world are cut —
no more will joy or sorrow come to my brow.

Hidden practice has now fulfilled my first intention;
from of old I have dwelt in mountains and never tired of depth.
Beyond emptiness, let a thousand ranges stand;
can a single speck of dust invade my eyes?
The pine wind ever speaks the Dharma of the un-born;
the flowing water rings long like an ancient lute.
Why trouble to raise the whisk again when entering the room?
The one who knows the tune will take it up at the moment.

In late spring the cold valley’s bamboo shoots put forth buds;
again I see the pine tips slowly come to flower.
A bowl is set out for the noon offering;
the monks all wait to taste the new tea.
Having nothing, I have no heavenly food by magic power;
being rich, I have the adornment of five-colored clouds.
I ask the singers and dancers of Chang’an:
how often have your dreams flown to a mountain household?

Through the three winter months I sit in dry Chan, wrapped in my robe;
gladly I see the spring light, loveliest of all.
A clay pot of wild greens is set as an offering;
the earth stove’s pine fire slowly ceases to smoke.
The green hills under snow open their faces again;
my white hair, kept against the cold, has reached my shoulders.
Fortunate to be a cloud-reclining guest in peaceful times,
I burn incense morning and night to bless the emperor’s years.

Old wanderings seem dimly a former life;
whenever I recall where I went and stayed, I am secretly startled.
These green hills today were that day’s dream;
this White Society now is the old vow.
To answer the moment I use only a steelyard without stars;
to amuse old age I keep only a broken-legged pot.
If you would ask the true meaning of the West’s coming:
the single stream of Caoxi brims full of water.

Dust gathers day by day on the rush mat and incense stand;
old and sick, this world-transcending body finds no room.
Into my dreams the spring’s voice comes clear to the ear;
to my bed the moonlight comes, cold and invading.
The idle mind does not join with any condition;
of pure karma only one true thought remains.
In the end, if you would know where you will come to rest —
the lotus has already tied the bond of lives to come.

A fire of old roots burning a thousand years;
a single-log bridge propping up the way.
No human trace comes or goes;
the hermit is placid, idly still.

The bright moon hangs in the cold sky;
its light pierces to the bottom of the cold pool.
Above and below were always the same;
seen thus, there is no this or that.

Moonlight is pale as water;
the tide is level, cold as emptiness.
A lone boat lies across a wild ford;
the man is in the midst of being and non-being.

We met by chance, and suddenly spoke of parting;
to meet again must be harder still.
Thinking of you, my heart is like snow;
my flying dream is cold at the Ji Gate.
